  "body": "The [timer initialization steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#timer-initialisation-steps) don't check whether the global's document is fully active. So per spec, calling `setTimeout()` on the `Window` of a removed iframe or of a navigated-away document returns a nonzero ID, and the timer task never runs. With #13010, the Trusted Types check (and so the default policy) also runs synchronously in that case.\n\nI tested with an iframe whose document has `require-trusted-types-for 'script'` and a default policy, capturing `iframe.contentWindow.setTimeout` first and then calling it as `setTimeout(\"...\", 0)` (no `this`, so it targets the old `Window`). No browser invoked the default policy, and they disagree on the rest:\n\n* Chrome Canary: removed iframe throws `Error` (\"The provided callback is no longer runnable.\"); navigated away returns 1, and the timer doesn't run.\n* Firefox Nightly: removed iframe returns 0; navigated away returns -1.\n* Safari TP: both throw a Trusted Types `TypeError` (\"This assignment requires a TrustedScript\").\n",
